\DOC LOCAL_NSQR_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_NSQR_TAC : int -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Discharges a call to the modular repeated-squaring subroutine inside a
machine-code {eventually} goal, advancing the symbolic state by one step and
replacing the pre-call register/memory assumptions with post-call ones.

\DESCRIBE
The single {int} argument {n} is the index of the new symbolic state: the
goal's current state variable is numbered {n-1} and the resulting state is
numbered {n}. Applied to a goal whose conclusion is an {eventually ...}
statement, it yields one subgoal in which the final state argument of
{eventually} is replaced by the new state variable with index {n}, the body of
the predicate being unchanged. On the assumption side, the block describing the
old state — the program-counter value, the argument registers set up for the
call, the loaded-code fact, the stack-pointer and saved-return-address facts,
the accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation, and the memory contents at the relevant
buffers — is removed and replaced by the corresponding facts about the new
state: a new program-counter value, the loaded-code, stack and return-address
facts restated at the new state, the surviving memory contents carried over, a
new assumption giving the contents of the destination buffer as a modular power
of the input, and an extended {MAYCHANGE} relation from the initial state to
the new state. Assumptions that do not mention the state (side conditions such
as non-overlapping and arithmetic facts) are kept unchanged at the front.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, exactly this one transformation occurs, always on goals
whose conclusion is such an {eventually} statement.

\ENDDOC
